  "oscar": {
   "paragraphs": [
    "There is a particular kind of silence that follows a **ZAP**. It is the silence of three people staring at a spot on the floor where, a moment ago, a four-year-old knight had been charging with his sword raised, shouting “FOR THE KING!” — and where there was now, apparently, nothing at all.",
    "Then the nothing sneezed.",
    "Oscar dropped to his knees. There, on the flagstones, no taller than a teaspoon, stood his brother. The colander had become a thimble. The wooden sword was a splinter. Roary, clutched under one arm, was the size of a jellybean, and appeared to be roaring about it.",
    "“Oh dear,” said Daddy. “Oh dear, oh dear, oh *dear*.” He knelt so carefully that both his knees cracked like walnuts. “Sam? Samuel? Can you hear me? Are you hurt? Is anything missing? Count your fingers. Count your *toes*.”",
    "“I am not *Samuel*,” came a voice like a bumblebee with opinions. “I am Samuel Lightning Thunderbolt Fire The Knight. And I am *fine*. I am *better* than fine. I am *tiny*.” He said it the way other people say *I am a millionaire*.",
    "Professor Pemberton Pinch had gone the colour of porridge. “Oh, bother,” he creaked, turning the glowing Shrink-u-lator over in his long fingers. “Oh, bother and blast. I didn’t mean — he *ran* at me. Nobody has ever run at me. Nobody has ever run *towards* me at all.” He slotted the gadget into a brass cradle on a great iron control panel by the wall, and it settled there with a heavy **CLUNK**. A little red light blinked on. “There. It locks itself after every zap. A safety feature. And the key,” he added, recovering a little of his creak, “is somewhere you will *never* find it.”",
    "Daddy had gone pink and damp around the edges, like a worried prawn. “Please,” he said. “He’s four. He has nursery on Monday. Well, not *this* Monday, we’re in America, but — please.”",
    "But the Professor had folded his arms and was pretending to examine the ceiling.",
    "Oscar thought very fast. He had once read, in a book about rescue dogs, that the most useful thing in any emergency is not strength, or speed, but a *small* creature who can get where big ones can’t. He looked at his brother. His brother looked back, with the enormous, gleaming eyes of a knight who has just been handed a quest.",
    "“Sam,” Oscar whispered, bending low. “He keeps it somewhere nearby. He glanced at that door when he said *never*. You’re small enough to go under it. I’ll keep him talking.”",
    "“Like a *spy* knight,” breathed Samuel.",
    "“Exactly like a spy knight.”",
    "~",
    "Oscar stood up, cleared his throat, and pointed at the nearest glass cabinet. “Excuse me, Professor. Is that *really* the Eiffel Tower? Only I read that it has one thousand, six hundred and sixty-five steps, and I’ve always wanted to know if that counts the little ones at the very top.”",
    "Something remarkable happened to Professor Pinch’s face. It was as if someone had opened all the curtains in a house that had been shut up for years.",
    "“It *does*,” he whispered. “It does count them. I counted them myself. With tweezers.” He hurried over, coat swishing. “And this — do you know what this is?”",
    "“Big Ben,” said Oscar. “Except Big Ben is technically just the name of the bell inside, not the tower. It weighs over thirteen tonnes. Well — it did.”",
    "The Professor clasped his hands together. “*Nobody* knows that. Nobody *ever* asks about my collection. And the Leaning Tower of Pisa, do you know how long —”",
    "“Almost two hundred years to build,” said Oscar. “And it started leaning before they’d even finished.”",
    "“*Before they’d even finished!*” cried the Professor, delighted, and Daddy, catching on, stroked his chin in a scholarly sort of way and said, “Fascinating. Truly. Very… scalable,” which made no sense at all but seemed to help.",
    "~",
    "Meanwhile, far below the conversation, Samuel Lightning Thunderbolt Fire The Knight had found his steed.",
    "The mouse-sized T. rex skeleton stood on a polished wooden plinth that had, for some museum reason, four tiny brass wheels. To anyone else it was an exhibit. To Samuel it was exactly, perfectly, magnificently the right size to *ride*. He scrambled up its tail, settled himself between its shoulder blades, and patted its skull.",
    "“Giddy-up,” he whispered. “Giddy-up, Rexy.”",
    "The old prison floor sloped gently towards a drain, as old prison floors do. Rexy began to roll. Then to rattle. Then to *fly*. **CLATTER-CLATTER-CLATTER** — under a bench, through a rusty pipe that rang like a bell, **WHOOSH** — out the other end and under the door in a single glorious skid.",
    "On the far side was a little kitchen that smelt of kippers and loneliness. A single teacup sat on the draining board. And on a cushion beneath the table lay the largest, fattest, grumpiest ginger cat Samuel had ever seen, snoring like a lawnmower.",
    "Above the cat, on a hook, hung a small brass key.",
    "To an ordinary person it would have been the size of a thumb. To Samuel it was a lance. He climbed Rexy’s skull, then a tea towel, then the hook itself, and lifted the key free. It was heavy and cold and exactly what a knight’s lance ought to feel like.",
    "One yellow eye opened beneath the table.",
    "“**Mrrrrrow?**”",
    "Samuel did not run. Knights do not run. He leapt onto Rexy, levelled his lance, held Roary up high, and the two of them gave the biggest roar in the history of very small things.",
    "“**ROOOOOOAR!**”",
    "The cat, who had never in nine lives been roared at by a dinosaur skeleton, shot straight up onto the top of the fridge and stayed there, bristling like a bottle brush.",
    "~",
    "Back in the great hall, Oscar was running out of facts. (This had never happened before, and he did not enjoy it.) Then he heard a faint rattle, and a squeak, and there was Samuel rolling out from under the door like a tiny triumphant parade, a brass key held high.",
    "Daddy scooped up the key while the Professor was busy telling Oscar about the Eiffel Tower’s paint (sixty tonnes of it, every seven years), and slid it into the control panel. **CLICK.** A brass flap swung open.",
    "Professor Pinch spun round. “No! Don’t — oh, *bother*.”",
    "Behind the flap were three switches, each sitting above a small brass plate with words engraved on it. On the left, a red square switch. In the middle, a blue triangular one. On the right, a round green one.",
    "“One of those,” said Professor Pinch, in a very small creak, “wakes the machine up properly. The other two…” He swallowed. “The other two shrink everything in this room. You. Me. The cat. *Everything*.”",
    "“Which one?” asked Oscar.",
    "“I — I’m not sure,” said the Professor miserably. “I made the plates years ago, as a puzzle, in case I ever forgot. And I forgot.”",
    "Daddy’s finger hovered over the panel. It was trembling. So was the rest of him.",
    "“Oh dear,” he whispered. “Oh dear, oh dear, oh *dear*.”"
   ],
   "puzzle": {
    "q": "Beneath the three switches, Oscar finds a card in the Professor’s spidery handwriting: “Only ONE of these plates tells the truth. Only ONE switch is safe.”\nThe red square switch’s plate says: “The safe switch is not the green one.”\nThe blue triangle switch’s plate says: “I am the safe switch.”\nThe green round switch’s plate says: “The blue switch is not safe.”\nWhich switch should Daddy press?",
    "a": "The green round switch. If red were safe, the red and green plates would both be true; if blue were safe, the red and blue plates would both be true — too many truths! Only if green is safe is exactly one plate (the green one) telling the truth."
   }
  }
